// Always-reserved screen space, independent of whatever the main view is
// currently showing -- the panel tools' key-hint bar, kept fixed regardless
// of which screen or overlay is active. See `apps/git/design.md`: "base
// commands always visible at the bottom".
//
// API
// ---
//   tuifooter.split(area, height)   the fixed band and what is left above it
//   tuifooter.Footer(spans)         a line of styled spans, drawn in that band
//
// There is deliberately no new drawing logic here beyond `split`: the footer
// IS a `tuiwidget.Line` (a row of styled spans), and what makes it a
// "persistent footer region" rather than just another line is that the
// caller cuts its band off the screen once, with `split`, and keeps drawing
// into it every frame regardless of what else is on screen -- the main view
// never gets those rows to lay out into, whether it is the outline, an
// overlay, or anything else this library or a program built on it adds
// later.

import tuibuf;
import tuigeom;
import tuistyle;
import tuiwidget;

/// Cut `area` into what is left for the rest of the screen and a fixed band
/// `height` rows tall at the bottom -- the footer's own, reserved regardless
/// of what the main view does with its own share. `height` is clamped to
/// `area` by `tuigeom`'s own rules, so a screen too short for it gives the
/// footer what rows there are rather than a negative height.
///
/// The short spelling of the one `tuigeom.rows` call every program with a
/// persistent footer would otherwise write for itself: `[main, footer]`.
pub List<tuigeom.Rect> split(tuigeom.Rect area, int height) {
    return tuigeom.rows(area, [tuigeom.Constraint.Fill(1), tuigeom.Constraint.Length(height)]);
}

/// A fixed line of styled spans -- the base commands, a status message,
/// whatever a program wants always on screen. An ordinary `Widget`, drawn
/// into the band `split` cuts off; nothing about it is footer-specific
/// beyond the name, which is the point -- any widget could go there, and
/// this is the common case of what does.
pub type Footer {
    pub List<tuiwidget.Span> spans;
    pub tuistyle.Style style = tuistyle.Style();
}

/// A footer of one plain span -- the common case of a single status line.
pub static Footer Footer.of(str text, tuistyle.Style style = tuistyle.Style()) {
    return Footer([tuiwidget.Span(text, style: style)], style: style);
}

pub void Footer.render(tuibuf.Buffer buf, tuigeom.Rect area) {
    tuiwidget.Line(spans, style: style).render(buf, area);
}
